course: dictionary
lesson: 3611
title: One word, several meanings: filing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Filing is a good example. The words around it, the context, tell you which meaning to use.
explain: «Iron filings sprinkled around a magnet line up and show its invisible magnetic field.» Here filings are tiny bits of metal. The clues are iron and magnet, so filings must be the small pieces of metal that a magnet pulls into lines.
explain: «The class was filing into the hall.» Filing here means walking in a line, one after another. Always ask: what is happening in the sentence? Then put your meaning in place of the word and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Filing means putting papers or computer documents in order in files or folders.
fact: Filings are tiny pieces rubbed off metal with a file, such as iron filings.
fact: Filing is also the -ing form of file, so it can mean walking in a line or smoothing something with a file.
fact: «Iron filings» show the pattern of a magnet's field: they line up, and the lines bunch up at the poles, where the pull is strongest.
fact: «Mixed iron filings and sulphur powder can be separated with a magnet.» A magnet picks out the iron filings, but not the new substance made when the mixture is heated.
exercise: «Iron filings sprinkled around a magnet line up.» What are iron filings? | Pupils walking in a line | *Tiny pieces of metal rubbed off with a file | Papers kept in a filing cabinet
exercise: In the office, Mia spends the afternoon filing papers. What does filing mean here? | Walking in a line | Smoothing wood with a file | Rubbing metal into tiny pieces | *Putting papers in order in files or folders
exercise: «The class was filing into the hall.» What does filing mean in this sentence? | Putting papers in folders | Sprinkling iron powder | Shouting loudly | *Walking in a line, one after another
test: «A magnet pulls iron filings into curved lines around it.» What are the filings in this sentence? | *Tiny bits of iron | Sheets of paper | Pupils in a line | Folders in a cabinet
test: Sam says filing always means keeping papers tidy. Which sentence shows he is wrong? | *The children filing out of the room | Mum files the bills in a folder | Ella files her nails with a gentle file | Dad files the documents on the computer
exam: Read «The teacher asked us to file into the hall, and then Tom spent the break filing his nails.» Which meaning of filing fits each use, and how do the words around it help you decide? | Rubbing metal into dust, then putting papers in folders, because the words file and nails sound alike | *Walking in a line, then smoothing with a file, because the words hall and nails are the clues | Putting papers in order, then walking in a line, because the words teacher and break are the clues | Walking in a line, then walking in a line again, because both sentences are about school
